// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/tauri-app.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeTauriApp(path: "/recipes/tauri-app", title: "Make a desktop or phone app with Tauri — Recipes", description: "Make a WebFluent project a Tauri app for the desktop or a phone: set it up, run it while you work, call Rust from a page, hear its events, package it.") {
    RecipeShell("Make a desktop or phone app with Tauri", summary: "This recipe ships a WebFluent project as a Tauri app: the build runs in the system's own web view, and a page calls a Rust side for what a browser cannot do — read a file on disk, open a folder dialog, show a notification. Nothing in the language changes; a page reaches Rust through a plain script under `src/`, with one typed function per command ([Call your own JavaScript](/recipes/call-your-own-script)). For a native side in JavaScript on Node.js instead, see [Make a desktop app with Electron](/recipes/electron-app).  You need a WebFluent project that builds with `wf build`, Rust, and the system libraries listed in Tauri's [prerequisites](https://v2.tauri.app/start/prerequisites/).  These are the settings a Tauri app needs. In `webfluent.app.json`:", outputs: ["desktop"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting"), Anchor(id: "variations", label: "Variations")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Build a Docker image", to: "/recipes/docker-image"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Check a deploy", to: "/recipes/check-a-deploy"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Deploy to Cloudflare Pages", to: "/recipes/deploy-to-cloudflare-pages")], source: "md-docs/recipes/tauri-app.md", how: true, hasVariations: true) {
        code {
            CodeBlock(#"{
    "build": { "compress": false },
    "meta": { "sitemap": false, "connect": ["ipc:", "http://ipc.localhost"] }
}"#, title: "json")
            Markdown("The commands that make the Rust side and run the app:")
            Terminal(lines: [
                        Line(command: "cargo install tauri-cli --version \"^2\" --locked"),
                        Line(command: "cargo tauri init --app-name notes --window-title Notes \\"),
                        Line(command: "    --frontend-dist ../build --dev-url http://localhost:3000 \\"),
                        Line(command: "    --before-dev-command \"wf serve\" --before-build-command \"wf build\""),
                        Line(command: "cargo tauri dev")
                    ])
            Markdown("And two keys in the `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json` that `cargo tauri init` writes:")
            CodeBlock(#"{
    "identifier": "com.example.notes",
    "app": { "withGlobalTauri": true }
}"#, title: "json")
        }
        variations {
            Heading("Run it on a phone", id: "run-it-on-a-phone").h3

            Markdown("The same project runs on Android and iOS. Make the phone's side once, then run it while you work (`ios` in place of `android`, on a Mac):")

            Terminal(lines: [
                        Line(command: "cargo tauri android init"),
                        Line(command: "cargo tauri android dev")
                    ])

            Markdown("- **The dev server.** `wf serve` listens to this machine only. To let the phone reach it, make `beforeDevCommand` `wf serve --host 0.0.0.0` and point `devUrl` at the machine's address on the network ([CLI](/reference/cli)).\n- **No window chrome.** A phone has no title bar: draw one only on a desktop.\n- **Edge to edge.** The page is drawn under the phone's own bars, so pad the app's frame by `env(safe-area-inset-*)`, once a script sets `viewport-fit=cover` on the viewport tag.\n- **Back.** Android's back gesture goes back through the router's history. To close an open sheet first, take the gesture with Tauri's `app.onBackButtonPress` from the same script, in an effect that gives it back.\n- **Plugins.** Capabilities are split by `\"platforms\"`, and a phone-only plugin (haptics, a barcode scanner) is registered under `#[cfg(mobile)]`.")

        }
        Markdown("The steps below take these settings in order, then add what a page calls: a Rust command, an event, a plugin.")

        Heading("Prepare the build for a desktop", id: "prepare-the-build-for-a-desktop").h3

        Markdown("The same project builds for the web and for a desktop. Two keys in `webfluent.app.json` leave out what only a website needs:\n\n- **`build.compress: false`** — the shell packs the folder into the app, so a `.gz` file beside every file would ship every file twice.\n- **`meta.sitemap: false`** — writes no `sitemap.xml` or `robots.txt`; nothing crawls an app. You can leave `meta.site_url` and the link-preview tags unset too.\n- **Leave `offline` out** — the files are already on the machine, so a service worker has nothing to keep.\n\n`meta.connect` is for the security policy; see [Allow Tauri requests in the security policy](#allow-tauri-requests-in-the-security-policy).\n\nA single-page build and a static build both work ([Static sites and SPAs](/about/static-or-single-page)), and addresses stay as they are. Tauri answers a path the build has no file for with `index.html`, so reloading the window on `/settings` shows the settings page.\n\nAn app is often used with no network, and a font named in `meta.fonts` comes from another origin. To make a font always available, put the file in `public/` and declare it with `@font-face` in a `.css` file under `src/` ([Styling](/learn/13-styling)).")

        Heading("Set up Tauri", id: "set-up-tauri").h3

        Markdown("1. `cargo install tauri-cli` installs the Tauri CLI, once per machine.\n2. `cargo tauri init`, run in the project's folder, writes `src-tauri/` beside `src/`: the Rust side, its `Cargo.toml` and `tauri.conf.json`. Its flags point Tauri at `build/` for the app, at `wf serve` on port 3000 while you work, and at the `wf` commands that make each. WebFluent never reads `src-tauri/`, and Tauri runs the two `wf` commands in the project's folder.\n3. Two keys in `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json` change:\n   - **`identifier`** — a reverse-domain name of your own. `cargo tauri build` refuses the default, `com.tauri.dev`.\n   - **`withGlobalTauri`** — puts Tauri's JavaScript API on `window.__TAURI__`, every plugin's API included, so a plain script uses it with no `import` and no npm.\n\nLeave `app.security.csp` at `null`: every page already carries its own policy (see [Allow Tauri requests in the security policy](#allow-tauri-requests-in-the-security-policy)).")

        Heading("Run the Tauri app while you work", id: "run-the-tauri-app-while-you-work").h3

        Markdown("`cargo tauri dev` runs `wf serve` and opens the app's window on it. Saving a `.wf` file rebuilds and reloads the window, and a build that fails shows its error over the page, as it does in a browser ([CLI](/reference/cli)). Saving a Rust file rebuilds the app. If `dev.port` is not `3000`, change `devUrl` to match.\n\nTo build the installers, run:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo tauri build")
            ])

        Markdown("This command runs `wf build`, embeds `build/` in the binary, and writes the installers under `src-tauri/target/release/bundle/`.")

        Heading("Call a Rust command", id: "call-a-rust-command").h3

        Markdown("A *command* is a Rust function a page may call. Write it in `src-tauri/src/lib.rs` and list it in `run()`. This file has two commands: `greet` returns a value, and `start_sync` returns at once and reports its progress as events:")

        CodeBlock(#"use tauri::Emitter;

#[tauri::command]
fn greet(name: &str) -> String {
    format!("Hello, {name}!")
}

#[tauri::command]
fn start_sync(app: tauri::AppHandle) {
    std::thread::spawn(move || {
        for done in [25, 50, 75, 100] {
            // … a quarter of the work …
            let _ = app.emit("sync-progress", done);
        }
    });
}

// in run(), before .run(…):
//     .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![greet, start_sync])"#, title: "rust")

        Markdown("A page calls a command through a script that names it. Write one function per command, typed with JSDoc, so `.wf` code calls `greet(name)` like any other function and the checker holds every call to its types:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/native.js
/**
 * Whether the page is inside the app, rather than a browser tab.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function inApp() {
  return "__TAURI__" in window;
}

/**
 * Call a Rust command. What its `Err` holds arrives as an `Error`.
 * @param {string} command
 * @param {object} [args]
 * @returns {Promise<any>}
 */
function invoke(command, args) {
  if (!inApp()) return Promise.reject(new Error("This needs the desktop app."));
  return window.__TAURI__.core.invoke(command, args).catch((err) => {
    throw new Error(typeof err === "string" ? err : JSON.stringify(err));
  });
}

/**
 * @param {string} name
 * @returns {Promise<string>}
 */
function greet(name) {
  return invoke("greet", { name });
}

/** Start syncing; how far it has got arrives as `sync-progress`. */
function startSync() {
  return invoke("start_sync");
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("Three rules hold for every command:\n\n- **Arguments are named.** A command's parameters are the keys of the object, in camelCase: a Rust `note_id` is `\{ noteId \}`.\n- **A result is a promise.** A command that returns `Result<T, E>` resolves with the `T`. Its `Err` rejects, and `invoke` turns it into an `Error`, so a page's `catch e` reads `e.message`.\n- **`inApp()` is false in a browser tab** — `wf serve` opened in a browser, `wf test`, `wf verify` — where there is no native side. Each function answers there instead of throwing, so every page still loads.\n\nThe page awaits the function in an action and shows the reply:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Hello(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "Says hello from Rust.") {
    state name = ""
    state reply = ""

    action ask() {
        try {
            reply = await greet(name)
        } catch e {
            reply = e.message
        }
    }

    Heading("Hello").h1
    if !inApp() {
        Alert("Open this in the app to talk to Rust.").info
    }
    Input(bind: name, label: "Your name")
    Button("Greet").primary { on click { ask() } }
    Text(reply)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("In the app, typing `Ada` and pressing **Greet** shows `Hello, Ada!`. In a browser tab, the page shows the alert, and **Greet** shows `This needs the desktop app.`")

        Heading("Listen for events from Rust", id: "listen-for-events-from-rust").h3

        Markdown("`start_sync` returns at once and reports as it goes, with `app.emit(…)`. A page hears an event for as long as it shows, so tie the listener to the page's lifetime: `mount:` starts it, and `cleanup:` stops it when the page, branch or item leaves. First, wrap Tauri's `listen` in a script:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/events.js
/**
 * Hear an event the native side emits, until the function it returns is called.
 * @param {string} name
 * @param {(payload: any) => void} handler
 * @returns {() => void}
 */
function onEvent(name, handler) {
  if (!inApp()) return () => {};
  const listening = window.__TAURI__.event.listen(name, (e) => handler(e.payload));
  return () => { listening.then((stop) => stop()); };
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("Then hand the listener an action. This page shows a progress bar that fills as the events arrive:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Sync(path: "/sync", title: "Sync", description: "How far the sync has got.") {
    state done = 0
    state problem = ""

    action setDone(n: Number) { done = n }
    action start() {
        try {
            await startSync()
        } catch e {
            problem = e.message
        }
    }

    Stack(gap: .md,
          mount: (n) => onEvent("sync-progress", setDone),
          cleanup: (stop) => stop()) {
        Heading("Sync").h1
        Progress(value: done, max: 100)
        Text("{done}%")
        Button("Sync now") { on click { start() } }
        Text(problem)
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Pressing **Sync now** moves the bar through 25, 50, 75 and 100%.")

        Heading("Use a Tauri plugin", id: "use-a-tauri-plugin").h3

        Markdown("A dialog, the file system, notifications, the clipboard, opening a link in the browser: each is a Tauri plugin. To add one, run the CLI. It adds the crate, the line in `run()`, and the plugin's default permission in `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo tauri add dialog")
            ])

        Markdown("With `withGlobalTauri` on, the plugin's API is on `window.__TAURI__.dialog`. Wrap what a page uses, as with a command:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/dialogs.js
/**
 * Ask for a folder; `null` when the reader cancels.
 * @returns {Promise<?string>}
 */
function pickFolder() {
  if (!inApp()) return Promise.resolve(null);
  return window.__TAURI__.dialog.open({ directory: true });
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("This page asks for a folder and shows the one the reader picked:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Library(path: "/library", title: "Library", description: "Where the notes are kept.") {
    state folder = ""

    action choose() {
        let picked = await pickFolder()
        if picked != null {
            folder = picked
        }
    }

    Heading("Library").h1
    Button("Choose a folder") { on click { choose() } }
    if folder != "" {
        Text("Notes are kept in {folder}.")
    } else {
        Text("No folder yet.").muted
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A plugin's call fails until its permission is granted. `cargo tauri add` grants the default set. Name anything beyond it — for example, a path the `fs` plugin may read — in the same capabilities file.")

        Heading("Allow Tauri requests in the security policy", id: "allow-tauri-requests-in-the-security-policy").h3

        Markdown("With `build.csp` on — as it is for a project `wf init` makes — every page says where it may send requests ([Security](/about/security)). Tauri sends each call as a request: to `http://ipc.localhost` on Windows and Android, and to `ipc://localhost` on macOS, Linux and iOS. Name both in `meta.connect`, the second as its scheme alone, which is how a policy names an app's own scheme:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "meta": { "connect": ["ipc:", "http://ipc.localhost"] } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("Other settings follow the same pattern:\n\n- **A protocol of your own** (`register_uri_scheme_protocol`) is named the same way. For a frame the app fills from it, write `\"frame\": [\"myapp:\", \"http://myapp.localhost\"]`; a frame's `src` may then use that scheme.\n- **WebAssembly**: a page that compiles it adds `\"wasm\": true`.\n- **Style elements**: a page whose scripts write `<style>` elements adds `\"style_elements\": true`.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("What to check").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every native call fails with `This needs the desktop app.`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The page is open in a browser tab, not the app — or `withGlobalTauri` is off, so `window.__TAURI__` is missing") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A Tauri call is blocked by the security policy")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`meta.connect` names both `ipc:` and `http://ipc.localhost`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A Tauri plugin's call fails")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Its permission is not granted in `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`cargo tauri build` refuses to run") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`identifier` in `tauri.conf.json` is still `com.tauri.dev`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The Tauri window shows nothing while you work")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`devUrl` does not match `dev.port`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The app's text falls back to another font offline")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The font comes from `meta.fonts`; put the file in `public/` and declare it with `@font-face`") }
                }
            }
        }

    }
}
